# Reading the original game (`terranx.exe`)

When a question is "what does SMAC do", answer it from the binary, not from the wiki or memory.

## Setup

- **Binary:** the GOG Alpha Centauri install, Alien Crossfire v2.0. Under Wine it is
  `~/.wine/drive_c/Program Files/GOG Galaxy/Games/Alpha Centauri/terranx.exe`. Check
  `sha1sum terranx.exe` = `4b19c1fe3266b5ebc4305cd182ed6e864e3a1c4a`. Every address below and in
  Thinker is for that build only.
- **Thinker** ([induktio/thinker](https://github.com/induktio/thinker)) supplies names for the
  binary's functions, globals and structs. Check it out into the gitignored `refs/` folder:

  ```bash
  git clone https://github.com/induktio/thinker refs/thinker   # first time
  git -C refs/thinker pull                                       # later
  ```

  Where to look in `refs/thinker/src/`: `engine.cpp` (address of every function and global),
  `engine_types.h` / `engine_base.h` (struct layouts), `base.cpp` and friends (`mod_*` rewrites
  of the original functions).
- **Disassembler:** `objdump -d -M intel --start-address=A --stop-address=B terranx.exe`. A
  function ends at the next address in Thinker's table. Write the output to a scratch file and
  grep it.

## Rules

- **Thinker is a lead, not the source.** Its `mod_*` functions follow the original flow but
  change some behaviour, marked `// Fix:`, and config options change more. Confirm every
  conclusion in the disassembly. Note where Thinker differs.
- **Check the map first.** [`docs/thinker/terranx-map.md`](docs/thinker/terranx-map.md) records
  what has already been confirmed, with addresses. Add to it whenever you confirm something.
  Record the address, what the code does, and where Thinker differs.
- **Branch direction is easy to misread.** A flipped `jl` / `jge` reads plausibly and tests
  written from the same reading won't catch it. For a rule that selects art, say which cell the
  extreme inputs should pick and confirm it from the art and an in-game screenshot.
- **Art palette values are shifted.** SMAC loads PCX art 10 slots up, so a byte the code writes
  is `palette.pcx` index + 10 (see `docs/thinker/smac-palette-lighting.md`).

## Deviations

[`config/deviations/README.md`](config/deviations/README.md) lists every way this game differs
from the binary. The map says what the binary and Thinker do; the deviations file says what we
do about it.

- **Record a deviation when it is found or decided**, not when it ships:
  - a binary bug (code that contradicts its own display, text or evident intent);
  - a Thinker `Fix:` or config option you add to the map;
  - a manual or wiki rule the binary does not implement;
  - a rule of our own that the binary does not have, from a plan, a review or the user.
- Follow the file's entry format. Every entry starts under **TODO**, with **Missing** naming
  what is absent. It moves to **Completed** only when every version (original, Thinker where it
  differs, ours) is playable:
  - code: each version implemented and selected by a config switch (`code-kinds.mdc`
    "Switches");
  - effects, config or Lua: each version implemented as a file, the non-default ones stored
    in `config/deviations/`. Swapping the file is the switch until variant profiles exist;
    engine work a file still needs (a missing Lua binding) is its own TODO entry.
  Shipping our version alone does not complete an entry. A Thinker change we play the binary's
  version of, with Thinker's unimplemented, goes under **Thinker changes not adopted**. No
  per-item status markers.
- **A plan that changes behaviour away from the binary** lists the deviations-file entry in its
  touch list.
- **Unshipped variant rule sets** (alternative effect lists, Lua formulas) live in
  `config/deviations/` and get a row in its Variant files table.
- Never ship a deviation silently. If the binary's behaviour is unknown, leave a TODO in the
  code and an entry under TODO here rather than inventing a rule.
